  subscription table you read from.

  - [1mImplicit only[0m — you're notified about tasks you created, are assigned, or commented on. Zero UI, works from day one,
    nothing to teach. But you can't stop caring without the relationship changing, and you can't start caring about a
    task you have no relationship to.
  - [1mExplicit only[0m — a watch button, nothing else. Fully predictable and fully controllable. But an empty inbox by
    default is the classic failure: nobody watches anything, so nobody gets notified, so the feature looks broken.
  - [1mHybrid (implicit + mute/watch overrides)[0m — auto-subscribed by relationship, with explicit watch to opt into anything
    and mute to opt out. This is what GitHub, Linear, and Asana all converged on, and the convergence isn't an
    accident.
  - [1mContainer-level[0m — subscribe to a whole project or list. Solves "I want to know about everything here," and composes
    with any of the above rather than replacing them.

  Cheap to change later: adding container-level subscriptions, adding new implicit relationships. Expensive to change
  later: starting derived-only and moving to a subscription table — every existing notification's audience becomes
  unreproducible, and you lose the mute history.

  My recommendation: [1mhybrid[0m, with the subscription record in the model from the start even though implicit rules
  populate most of it. The storage cost is trivial and it's the one piece here that's genuinely painful to retrofit. I'd
  leave container-level subscriptions out of v1 — YAGNI until you have projects worth subscribing to.
